  "query_preview": "..\n\n - Meteorological Overview:\n\nNorthwest flow aloft remains the dominant upper-level pattern over \nthe next few days as a ridge builds along the West coast. With the \nridge gradually building through the week, temperatures will also \ngradually build throughout the week. Dry PWATs along with weak \nforcing will keep the region dry through the week.\n\nHeading into Friday and the weekend, this ridge begins to flatten, \nincreasing winds aloft. Deep daytime mixing will allow these \nbreezy winds to mix down to the surface Friday and Saturday. Deep \nmixing will also help bring afternoon humidities down into the \nteens. The hot, breezy, and dry condtions will allow for an \nincrease in fire weather to elevated to critical. \n\nWesterly flow aloft persists into the beginning of next week, but \nlow end chances chances for precipitation is trending mostly \ndrier until better forcing from a trough moves in \nTuesday/Wednesday time frame.\n\n - Forecast Confidence & Scenarios:\n\nOne thing that remains uncertain is the timing and location of the \nbreezier winds Friday. Some solutions have a broader 700mb jet \nFriday afternoon, some have a more localized jet, and some push \noff the breezier winds to Saturday. This affects the overall fire \nweather risk threat. However, there remains good confidence for at\nleast more elevated fire weather Friday and Saturday due to the \ncombination of dry, hot, and breezy conditions. Where the better \nwinds set up will determine the areas more at risk for critical \nfire weather. -Wilson",
